Here's a clean, semantic structure:

```html
<article class="product-card">
  <img src="product.jpg" alt="Product Name" class="product-card__image">
  <div class="product-card__content">
    <h3 class="product-card__title">Product Name</h3>
    <p class="product-card__price">$29.99</p>
    <button class="product-card__button">Add to Cart</button>
  </div>
</article>
```

And the CSS:

```css
.product-card {
  background: white;
  border-radius: 8px;
  box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
  overflow: hidden;
}

.product-card__image {
  width: 100%;
  height: 200px;
  object-fit: cover;
}

.product-card__content {
  padding: 1rem;
}

.product-card__title {
  margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  font-size: 1.1rem;
}

.product-card__price {
  color: #2563eb;
  font-weight: bold;
  font-size: 1.25rem;
  margin: 0 0 1rem;
}

.product-card__button {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 0.75rem;
  background: #2563eb;
  color: white;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 4px;
  cursor: pointer;
  font-size: 1rem;
}

.product-card__button:hover {
  background: #1d4ed8;
}
```

I'm using BEM naming convention here. Would you like me to explain why BEM is useful for component-based CSS?

The placeholder works! Now I want to add a hover effect to the card. Can you review my attempt?

```css
.product-card:hover {
  transform: translateY(-5px);
  box-shadow: 0 8px 25px rgba(0,0,0,0.15);
}
```

Your hover effect looks good! Just one important addition - you should add a `transition` property so the effect is smooth rather than instant:

```css
.product-card {
  background: white;
  border-radius: 8px;
  box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
  overflow: hidden;
  /* Add this for smooth animation */
  transition: transform 0.2s ease, box-shadow 0.2s ease;
}

.product-card:hover {
  transform: translateY(-5px);
  box-shadow: 0 8px 25px rgba(0,0,0,0.15);
}
```

**Tips:**
- `0.2s` is a good duration - fast enough to feel responsive, slow enough to be noticeable
- `ease` gives a natural acceleration/deceleration
- Only transition the properties you're changing for better performance
- Consider adding `will-change: transform` if you notice any jankiness (but use sparingly)

Your shadow values are well chosen - the increased blur radius (25px vs 8px) and opacity create a nice "lifted" effect!
